# rules/generated/wednesday_heartbleed.rules
# Generated detection for the Wednesday 2017 Heartbleed (CVE-2014-0160) exploitation.
#
# CONFIRMED THREAT — true positive.
# Attacker 172.16.0.1 (known CIC-IDS2017 external attacker) exploited a vulnerable
# OpenSSL TLS service on victim 192.168.10.51:444.
#
# Evidence (logs/wednesday-raw/eve.json):
#   - 2017-07-06 03:42:20, flow 172.16.0.1:45022 -> 192.168.10.51:444
#     flow.bytes_toserver = 1,963,593   (1.9 MB of crafted heartbeat requests)
#     flow.bytes_toclient = 81,482,031  (81.5 MB returned to the attacker)
#   - A normal TLS handshake on the same service is ~350-850 B up / ~0.4-1.6 KB down.
#     The 81.5 MB response is the out-of-bounds memory disclosure: repeated crafted
#     Heartbeat requests leaking ~64 KB of server process memory each.
#   - ET 2018377 "Possible OpenSSL HeartBleed Large HeartBeat Response" fired ONCE
#     (192.168.10.51:444 -> 172.16.0.1:45022); SURICATA 2230014 "invalid encrypted
#     heartbeat" fired twice. The stock heartbeat-payload rules are unreliable
#     because the heartbeat record is encrypted, so most of the exploitation is
#     invisible to the stock ruleset.
#   - Immediately before the exploit there were repeated short TLS handshakes to
#     :444 (03:22:55, 03:23:13, 03:29:47, 03:39:50, 03:39:54, 03:40:02, 03:40:08,
#     03:42:15) — the attacker was fingerprinting the vulnerable service (identical
#     JA3 7c657f99ee40e9602437b4c56bcc4c16).
#
# NOTE: cumulative flow volume (the decisive 81.5 MB leak) cannot be matched by a
# single packet rule. These rules anchor on the app-layer heartbeat anomaly plus
# the recon burst that preceded exploitation.

# --- 1. Heartbleed heartbeat anomaly, direction-scoped to the leak -----------
# Suricata's TLS parser saw an invalid/encrypted heartbeat. Scoped to_server
# (external client -> HOME_NET service) so the offending direction is explicit,
# complementing stock 2230014 which alerts in both directions.
alert tls $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any ( \
    msg:"CUSTOM TLS Heartbleed exploitation attempt - invalid encrypted heartbeat to HOME_NET TLS service (CVE-2014-0160)"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    app-layer-event:tls.invalid_encrypted_heartbeat; \
    classtype:attempted-user; reference:cve,2014-0160; reference:url,heartbleed.com; \
    sid:7600101; rev:2;)

# --- 2. Sustained TLS handshake burst from one external host -----------------
# The attacker opened repeated short TLS handshakes to the SAME HOME_NET TLS
# service before the big leak. In this capture the final 5-minute window
# (03:39:50-03:44:50) contains 5 handshakes, so count 5 / 300s catches it.
alert tls $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any ( \
    msg:"CUSTOM TLS heartbeat/handshake burst to HOME_NET service (Heartbleed recon/exploit precursor)"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 5, seconds 300; \
    classtype:attempted-recon; reference:cve,2014-0160; \
    sid:7600102; rev:2;)

# --- 3. TLS service on a non-standard port touched by external recon ---------
# Legitimate HOME_NET TLS services are on 443/8443. The vulnerable service here
# sat on TCP/444. Flag external clients repeatedly probing a non-standard TLS
# port (including 444). Count 5 / 300s matches the observed pre-exploit burst.
alert tls $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET ![443,8443] ( \
    msg:"CUSTOM TLS service on non-standard port handshaked from external host (possible vulnerable-service recon)"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 5, seconds 300; \
    classtype:attempted-recon; \
    sid:7600103; rev:2;)
